window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
["why.htm","","1","Why Without Python","Almost all machine learning today is written in Python. In this chapter we look at why that is, what it costs, and why this book takes another road."],
["why.htm","why-two","1.1","Two Languages","A neural network computes a great many very simple things: multiplications, additions, a tanh here and there. Written in Python, each of them is slow, because Python looks again at every number to see what kind of value it is and what a plus sign means for it. That is why hardly anyone computes in Python itself. The work is done by libraries written in C, C++ or CUDA, NumPy for matrices, PyTorch and JAX for whole networks, and Python only tells them what to do. That works well as long as everything you have in mind fits into the calls of these libraries. Whoever needs a loop they do not have writes it in the second language, in C++ or CUDA, and connects the two. Then you work in two languages, and what is actually computed is written in the one you do not have in front of you. How big the difference is shows in the program from chapter 3 that learns a circle: once in Python, loop by loop as in Scaly, once with NumPy, where each layer is one line, and once in Scaly. All three learn the same network on the same points from the same starting weights. Measured are 5000 learning steps on a MacBook Pro with an M1 Pro: VersionTimePython, loop by loop24.6 sPython with NumPy0.25 sScaly0.10 s Python alone takes 250 times as long as Scaly. NumPy makes up almost all of it, although our matrices are so small that with every call the way from Python into the library costs more than the computation itself. The numbers the programs print along the way are the same in Python and Scaly up to the 200th step, digit for digit; after that the last places differ. Python takes tanh from the system's C library, Scaly its own, and the two differ in the last place in about one case in two hundred; then Scaly's result is mostly the one closer to the exact value. NumPy adds up the sums in another order and differs in the last place from the first step on. After the 500 steps of the programs all three get the same 188 of 200 test points right, after the 5000 of the measurement all 200. The three programs are on GitHub."],
["why.htm","why-one","1.2","One Language Down to the Card","In Scaly there is no second language. A loop you write is translated through LLVM into machine code, like a C program; it is fast because it is fast itself, not because a library replaces it. The program you read is the program that computes. And it scales by itself. A for whose rounds are independent of each other Scaly spreads over the processor's cores and onto the graphics card where that pays, without anything about it in the program. No garbage collector clears the memory: every call frees what it made when it returns. We look at both more closely in chapter 3. And the same programs also run in the browser: the pages beside this book compute with Scaly programs built for WebAssembly."],
["why.htm","why-cost","1.3","What We Give Up","That has its price. Around Python a large store has grown: ready networks, trained weights, libraries for every data format, tools to look at it all. We can take none of it. We write every piece ourselves, from the single neuron to the learning step, and that is exactly what this book is about: what a network does is then in front of you, in the text and in the program. In speed, too, the big libraries are ahead where they are hand-tuned for one task. A matrix product of 2048 rows and columns takes NumPy 9 milliseconds on this Mac, with Apple's Accelerate library; the plain loop from chapter 3 takes 0.27 seconds for it on the graphics card. We will take on that gap later: we write the matrix product better ourselves and measure it against the manufacturers' libraries."],
["field.htm","","2","A First Model","In this chapter we develop a program that can tell two kinds of points apart. We begin with a single neuron and then build, from layers of neurons, a small network that finds its own weights."],
["field.htm","field-points","2.1","Two Kinds of Points","Let there be a plane with the coordinates x and y, each from -6 to +6. Into this plane we put points of two kinds: orange and blue. In the programs the kind of a point is represented by a number: 1 for orange and -1 for blue. A model is a function that computes, from the coordinates x and y of a place in this plane, a guess of the kind of a point at that place: a value between -1 and +1. At -1 the model is sure that a blue point lies there; at +1 it predicts an orange one with certainty. Values in between express uncertainty: the sign says which kind the model leans to, and the nearer the value is to 0, the less sure it is; at 0 it cannot decide. We could write such a function any way we like, for example f⁡(x,y)=x6The plane coloured by f(x, y) = x / 6: blue on the left, orange on the right, pale in the middle. It is +1 at the right edge, -1 at the left and 0 on the upright axis: on the right it guesses orange, on the left blue, and the nearer the middle, the less sure it is. Or f⁡(x,y)=1−x2+y236The plane coloured by f(x, y) = 1 − (x² + y²)/36: an orange disc in the middle, blue corners. It is +1 at the centre, −1 in the four corners, because there x2+y2=36+36=72, and 0 on the circle of radius 6 around the centre, where x2+y2=36. Whether such a function can predict the points' kinds well depends on where they lie: the first suits points that are orange on the right and blue on the left, the second an orange disc in a blue ring. Machine learning, on the other hand, does not make the formula up. It fixes only its general form, a formula with numbers left open, the parameters. In the second example the 36 could be such an open number, f⁡(x,y)=1−(x2+y2)/c, and c would decide how large the orange disc is; where the formula falls below −1, it counts as −1. Which values the parameters get, a program finds out from examples, points whose kind is known. Finding them is what this book calls learning, and a function with its parameters found a trained model. What an open number does, you can try for yourself: open the page with the model f⁡(x,y)=1−(x2+y2)/c and move c back and forth. The colouring follows the formula: a large c makes the orange disc large, a small one small."],
["field.htm","field-neuron","2.2","One Neuron","The simplest form of such a function is a neuron. It multiplies each of its inputs by a number of its own, its weight, adds them up and adds one more number, its bias: z=w1x+w2y+b The result z is called the neuron's weighted sum. Where it is positive the neuron guesses orange, where it is negative, blue. The points where z is exactly 0 form a straight line, so a neuron parts the plane by a line: the weights turn it, the bias moves it. Now let us try to adjust the neuron so that it can tell, as well as possible, the colour of six points we scatter over the plane at will: three orange and three blue. We begin with the weights 1 and 1 and the bias 0: z=x+yThe plane parted by the line x + y = 0, orange to the upper right, blue to the lower left; six points, a dark ring around an orange one on the blue side. Five of the six lie on the right side; the one in the ring is orange but lies below the line. With w2=0 the line turns to the upright axis, and all six are right: z=xThe plane parted by the upright axis, orange on the right, blue on the left; all six points on the side of their colour. Changing the weights and the bias step by step until the guesses are right is the essence of what we shall call learning. Open the page with the neuron and pull at w1, w2 and b: the line turns and moves, and the page counts how many of the six points lie right. With a click and a right click you can put down points of your own as well. Below the picture stands the function in Scaly that the page computes for it."],
["field.htm","field-loss","2.3","How Wrong","To change the weights by itself a program needs a measure of how wrong the neuron is. This measure is a number that gets smaller as the neuron gets better. Counting the wrong guesses does not serve: a small change of a weight usually changes no guess at all, so the count does not say which way to turn. So we first map the weighted sum z to a number between −1 and 1 by a smooth function, here the hyperbolic tangent: a=tanh⁡(z)The curve of tanh: near −1 on the left, rising through the origin and coming ever closer to 1 on the right; dashed, the lines at 1 and −1. With this function the model can answer as the beginning of the chapter asked: near +1 or -1 where it is sure, near 0 where it is not. Such a function we shall call the neuron's activation function, and a its answer for a particular point of the plane. Now the answer can be compared with the kind t the point really has: L=12(a−t)2 L is the loss of one point: 0 where the answer is exactly the kind, larger the further it is from it, and every small change of a weight changes it a little. The mean of the losses of all points is the neuron's loss, and that is the measure we were looking for. The hyperbolic tangent is not the only possible activation function. In large networks one mostly takes an even simpler function today, ReLU: max⁡(0,z), that is z itself where z is positive and 0 elsewhere. Its slope is always 1 for positive z, while that of tanh becomes nearly 0 far out. What that means for learning shows in the next section."],
["field.htm","field-gradient","2.4","Downhill","So the loss of our neuron is a function of the three values w1, w2 and b. Now we want to find the values for which it becomes as small as possible. Trying every combination is out of the question: each of the three values can be any number, so there are infinitely many. Even if we tried only 100 numbers for each value, that would be 1003, a million combinations, and for each we would have to compute the loss over all points. For three values a computer still manages that, but the network for XOR that we build later already has 13 numbers and so 10013=1026 combinations, and large networks have millions of weights. So we begin with some values and move from there step by step in the direction in which the loss falls fastest, with all three values at once. Which direction that is can be read off each value separately: how much does the loss change when the value changes a little? That is the partial derivative of the loss with respect to that value, for w1 written ∂L/∂w1. It is partial because only this one value changes while the others are held fixed; the round ∂ instead of a d marks that. The loss does not depend on w1 directly. It depends on the answer a, the answer on the sum z, and the sum on w1. Each link of that chain has a derivative that is easy to work out: How much does the loss change when the answer changes? ∂L∂a=a−t, the derivative of 12(a−t)2.How much does the answer change when the sum changes? ∂a∂z=1−a2, the derivative of tanh.How much does the sum change when w1 changes? ∂z∂w1=x, because w1 is multiplied by x. The chain rule says that the derivative of the whole chain is the product of the derivatives of its links: ∂L∂w1=∂L∂a·∂a∂z·∂z∂w1=(a−t)(1−a2)x For w2 the last factor is y, for the bias it is 1. Added up over all points and divided by their number, these partial derivatives are the gradient: the direction in which the loss rises fastest. One step of gradient descent moves each of the three values a little the other way, w1←w1−η∂L∂w1 where the small number η is the learning rate. And when does one stop? When the loss is small enough, or when it hardly falls any more. The pages of this chapter stop as soon as the loss is below 0.02 or has fallen by less than 0.0001 over a hundred steps. Such a rule is called a stopping criterion. Open the page where the neuron learns. It begins with values that guess badly: only two of the six points are right. Press “One step” several times in a row: w1, w2 and b move a bit each time, the line turns, the loss falls. Press “Learn”: after a good twenty steps all six are right; after a good eighty the loss is below 0.02, and learning stops. w2 is then nearly 0, as in our attempt by hand. With the learning rate 0.01 it goes much more slowly. Below the picture stands the step in Scaly. Try the start values w1=w2=−3 as well (the page with these values): then the neuron hardly moves, and after a hundred steps the page stops, since the loss no longer falls. Nearly all points lie on the wrong side, and far from the line, where tanh is nearly ±1 and hardly rises any more. Since the factor 1−a2 is nearly 0 there, so is the gradient: a neuron that is sure and wrong hardly learns. This is called saturation, and it is why one begins with small weights."],
["field.htm","field-features","2.5","Better Inputs","Our neuron parts the plane by a line. But some arrangements of points cannot be parted by a line, for example a disc of orange points inside a ring of blue ones: every line then has orange and blue on both sides. Open the page where the neuron learns and put down unfavourable points yourself: some orange ones in the middle and a ring of blue ones around them. A click puts down an orange point, a right click a blue one, a click on a point takes it away. Press “Learn”: the neuron finds no line that fits, settles for a plane that is unsure everywhere, and stops, since the loss no longer falls. What the neuron lacks is not skill at sums but the right view of the points. Let us give it the squares of x and y as its inputs instead: z=w1x2+w2y2+b With w1=w2=−1 and b=9, say, z is 0 exactly where x2+y2=9, on the circle of radius 3 around the centre. Inside, z is positive, outside negative: z=−x2−y2+9The plane with an orange circle of radius 3 around the centre, blue elsewhere; the orange points of the disc lie inside the circle, the blue ones of the ring outside. Why can the neuron do this all of a sudden? Let us call its new inputs u=x2 and v=y2. Then z=w1u+w2v+b is a straight line again, only not in the plane of x and y but in that of u and v. Drawn there, the disc lies near the origin and the ring further out, and a line parts them: z=−u−v+9The same points over u = x² and v = y²: the orange ones near the origin below a slanting line, the blue ones further out above it. So the neuron still draws a line, but a line in the inputs it is given; on the plane of the points it becomes a circle. Choosing what a model sees is called feature engineering, and good inputs can make a hard problem easy. Open the page with the neuron given x² and y². It begins with the disc and the ring and guesses badly at first. Press “Learn”: after a good twenty steps all points are right, and the orange area settles around the disc; after a good two hundred the loss is below 0.02. Here too you can put down points and take them away."],
["field.htm","field-hidden","2.6","Hidden Layers","For a disc the right inputs are easy to guess; for most data they are not. So we leave it to neurons to compute the inputs. It is this idea that makes neural networks strong. Let us take several neurons instead of one. Each of them is given the same inputs x and y, but has weights and a bias of its own and so draws a line of its own; its answer says on which side of it a point lies. Neurons that read the same inputs like this form a layer. Now we form one more layer. It is given not x and y but the answers of the neurons of the first layer, and here it consists of a single neuron, the output. So the computation runs in two stages: from x and y the first layer computes its answers, and from those answers the output computes its own. From outside one sees only the inputs and the output, so the layer in between is called a hidden layer; its answers are intermediate results. The data that shows it best is called XOR: orange at the upper right and the lower left, blue at the other two corners. No single line parts them. Two neurons in a hidden layer can, if each cuts off one of the orange corners. Let their answer here simply be the sign sgn of their weighted sum, +1 or −1. The first says +1 only at the upper right: h1=sgn⁡(x+y−3)The plane with the points of XOR, orange only beyond the line x + y = 3 at the upper right, blue elsewhere. the second only at the lower left: h2=sgn⁡(−x−y−3)The plane with the points of XOR, orange only beyond the line x + y = −3 at the lower left, blue elsewhere. The output reads the two answers. If either of them says +1, z=h1+h2+1 is +1, so orange; if both say −1, it is −1, so blue: z=h1+h2+1The plane with the points of XOR: orange at the upper right and lower left corners, a blue band between them; every point on the side of its colour. How the output manages that shows when each point is put where the hidden layer takes it. The output is a neuron like our first one, only its inputs are called h1 and h2 instead of x and y. So it does not see the plane of the points but a new plane with the axes h1 and h2, and in it each point has the place (h1,h2): the three orange points at the upper right land at (+1,−1), the three at the lower left at (−1,+1), and all six blue ones at (−1,−1). In this plane a single line parts the two kinds, and that line is the one the output draws: h1+h2+1=0The plane of the answers h1 and h2: an orange point at (+1, −1), one at (−1, +1), a blue one at (−1, −1); the line h1 + h2 + 1 = 0 parts the blue one at the lower left from the orange ones. The output joins the two lines into an or: orange where the first or the second neuron says yes. Two lines and a neuron that joins them do what one line alone cannot. With more neurons and more layers, borders of any intricacy can be built this way. Open the page with a network of one hidden layer. It has three hidden neurons and begins on the points of XOR with small weights that guess only half of the points right. Press “Learn”: after nearly thirty steps all twelve are right, after a good hundred the loss is below 0.02, and learning stops. The small pictures show which line each hidden neuron has found; the output joins them. Further down the page the network stands in layers, one above the other, with the weights as lines; drag it with the mouse to turn it. How the network finds its thirteen numbers is the next section’s subject. The activation function matters here. If each neuron simply passed on its weighted sum, the output would add up sums of sums, which is again only a weighted sum of x and y, so again only a line. Only the activation function, which bends each neuron's sum, lets the layers do more than one neuron could. In our example by hand that is the sign; in the network from the box above, the hyperbolic tangent."],
["field.htm","field-backprop","2.7","Backpropagation","How does a hidden neuron learn? Its weights are as far from the loss as can be, behind the output neuron. The chain rule still works, with a chain one link longer. The clever part is the order in which it is computed. First everything is computed forward, as before: each hidden neuron's answer hj, then the output's sum and its answer a. Then backward. The output neuron's share of the error, the loss by its sum, is the familiar d=(a−t)(1−a2) and its weights' derivatives follow from it as for a single neuron: dhj for the weight vj that reads hidden neuron j. Hidden neuron j reaches the loss through that weight, so its answer's slope is dvj, and through its own tanh its share of the error becomes dj=dvj(1−hj2) From there its weights are once more a single neuron's: djx, djy and dj for its bias. The error is handed back through the network layer by layer, each layer's share computed from the share of the layer after it. That is backpropagation, and it is the whole secret of how networks of any depth learn. Open the page with the network for XOR once more. Below the picture there is now the procedure network_step: backpropagation as derived here. For each point it computes forward the answers hj and a, then back d and from it each dj, and at the end it moves each of the thirteen numbers a little against its gradient. Each press of One step is one call of this procedure. The hidden neurons on the page start from different weights on purpose. Had they all started alike, they would answer alike, get the same gradient in every step and stay alike forever, three copies of one line. That is why a network usually starts from random weights. A network needs nothing more to learn, however many layers it has: compute forward, hand the error back, move each weight a little against its gradient."],
["field.htm","field-field","2.8","The Field","The networks in our boxes were small and each built for one task. The field is a page on which you put a network together yourself and watch it learn. You choose the points, the inputs, how many hidden layers it has and how many neurons in each, and how it learns. It computes exactly as the last sections derived. At the top of the page lies a bar with the buttons. Below it stand three parts side by side, from left to right the data, the network and the output; on a narrow screen they stand one below the other. The picture shows the page with the spiral, after a little over 2000 steps. The field: at the top the bar with Pause, One step, Start again, the count of the steps, learning rate, activation function, regularization and speed; below it on the left the data with the spiral chosen, in the middle the network with the seven inputs, two hidden layers of six and four neurons and the output, on the right the two losses, their course and the plane with the points of the spiral. The barPause stops the network; the same button is then called Learn and lets it learn on. While it is stopped, One step takes exactly one step of gradient descent over all the points it learns from. Start again gives the network new random weights and keeps the points. Beside them the page counts the steps since the start.The Learning rate is the one from the section “Downhill”, to choose from 0.001 to 3. Under Activation function there are four to choose from, for the neurons of the hidden layers: the hyperbolic tangent, ReLU, the sigmoid function σ⁡(z)=11+e−z, which runs between 0 and 1 instead of between −1 and +1, and none. Without an activation function each hidden neuron simply hands on its weighted sum, and the whole network, however many layers it has, draws only one line. The output neuron always answers with the hyperbolic tangent. Regularization (L2) is explained in the next section.The Speed says how many steps the network takes between two pictures, in five levels from 1 to 100. The page draws a picture every 30 milliseconds at most.The dataFive small pictures choose the points: Circle, a disc inside a ring; XOR, the four quarters of the plane, the kind by the sign of xy; Clouds, two heaps around (2,2) and (−2,−2); Spiral, two arms wound into each other; and Your own, an empty plane. Into it you put points yourself, an orange one with a click, a blue one with a shift-click or a right-click. For the other four the page draws 300 points, half of each kind.The Noise slider moves each point at random, up to 50 %, so that the kinds reach into each other; for the clouds it makes the heaps wider instead. New points draws the same arrangement anew. A change of data only exchanges the points; the network learns on with the weights it has.The networkThe network stands in columns, from left to right in the order in which it is computed. The first column holds the seven possible inputs: x, y, x2, y2, xy, sin⁡x and sin⁡y. You know the squares from the section “Better Inputs”; new are the sines of the two coordinates, which go up and down a little less than twice across the plane. A click on an input gives it to the network or takes it away. One taken away is pale and struck through, and one always stays.Then come the hidden layers, each a column. With − and + above the network you take a layer away or add one, up to six; with − and + above a column, a neuron of that layer, up to eight. Without a hidden layer the network is a single neuron as at the beginning of the chapter. The last column is the output neuron.Each neuron, each input too, is a small square. It shows what the neuron answers over the whole plane, orange where the answer is positive, blue where it is negative, like our pictures for XOR. The lines between the squares are the weights: orange positive, blue negative, the thicker, the larger their size. On each line a light runs from left to right, the faster, the stronger the weight. When the network's shape, its inputs or its activation function change, it starts over from new random weights.The outputAt the top stand two numbers, the Training loss and the Test loss, below them their course since the last start: the training loss solid, the test loss dashed. What training and test points are is explained in the next section.The large picture is the plane, from −6 to 6 both ways, with all the points. Beneath them lies the output neuron's answer at every place, the stronger the colour, the nearer to +1 or −1. Only the decision shows instead only on which side of zero the answer lies, and with it the border the network draws. Show the test points shows and hides the ringed points. Below the three parts the page says in a few sentences what happens, and at the very bottom the program can be unfolded that the last section of this chapter is about. The links in the boxes set the page up to fit. What they choose stands in the address after the #. Without such settings the page starts with the circle, the inputs x and y and two hidden layers of four and two neurons. Open the field with the spiral, only x and y as inputs and one hidden layer of four neurons. The network starts learning at once, but its loss falls only slowly and stays high: four lines are not enough for the spiral. Then click sin x and sin y in the column of the inputs. The network starts again, and after a few seconds its loss has fallen to a fraction."],
["field.htm","field-test","2.9","Generalization","So far the network has learned from all the points, and we have only asked how well it meets those very points. But a model is meant to place points rightly that it has never seen as well. That is why the field splits its points into two halves. The network learns from the one: these are the training points, and their mean loss, the training loss, is the loss gradient descent makes smaller. The other half, the test points, drawn with a ring, the field holds back: the network never learns from them. Their mean loss, the test loss, says how well the network generalizes. When the data is noisy and the network large, the two losses part ways. The network learns the training points ever better, their noise included, and draws borders around single stray points. The test loss then stops falling and rises again. That is overfitting. One remedy is regularization: for every weight w a penalty λw2 is added to the loss. λ is a small number we choose. Large weights become expensive. The penalty's derivative is 2λw, so every step of gradient descent pulls every weight a little towards zero. Smaller weights give smoother borders, because a neuron with small weights goes over from −1 to +1 only slowly. Because the penalty is the square of the weights, this form is called L2 regularization. Open the spiral with 30 % noise and four hidden layers of eight. After some seconds the training loss is far below the test loss, and while the training loss keeps falling, the test loss rises. The plane shows islands around single points. Choose 0.01 under Regularization (L2) and click Start again: the penalty keeps the weights small. The training loss stays higher, but the test loss stays well below the one before and hardly rises any more, and the borders are smoother."],
["field.htm","field-program","2.10","The Field's Program","Behind the page stands one Scaly program, field.scaly, built for WebAssembly. It is the program of the page with the network for XOR grown up: everything the page lets you set, and the backpropagation that network_step writes out for a network with one hidden layer, here in loops over any number of layers. The numbers the page shows it reads straight out of the program's memory, without a copy. The picture of the plane is a for over its 6400 points, which Scaly spreads over the cores of the machine by itself. The same program also runs without the page, on the command line: field 0 500 lets the network with hidden layers of four and two neurons learn the circle for 500 steps, writes down the two losses every hundred steps and at the end how many of the 200 test points it places rightly. Its source is on GitHub. Everything this chapter derived by hand, the chain rule through every link and the gradient of every weight, a program can derive too. That is the subject of the chapters ahead: first how to write the computation for all points at once, with matrices, and how Scaly holds these many numbers and computes with them fast; then a tape that records the forward computation and plays it back to get every derivative."],
["matrices.htm","","3","Many Points at Once","Our network computes point by point so far. In this chapter we write the same computation for all points at once, with matrices, and see how Scaly holds the many numbers and computes with them fast."],
["matrices.htm","matrices-rows","3.1","A Row for Each Point","A neuron forms its weighted sum for one point, z=w1x+w2y+b. For the 200 training points of the circle those are 200 sums, and a layer of four neurons forms 800. Writing the inputs of all points one below the other, a row for each point, gives a table of 200 rows and 2 columns, a matrix X. The layer's weights we write as a matrix W too, a column for each neuron, so 2 rows and 4 columns. The sum of neuron j for point i is then row i of X with column j of W, multiplied number by number and added up, plus the neuron's bias: zij=xi1w1j+xi2w2j+bjThree blocks of cells: on the left X, a row for each point and two columns, on top W with two rows and a column for each neuron, below it Z. One row of X and one column of W are orange, and where they cross in Z the one number they make together is strong orange. All these sums together are a matrix again, Z, a row for each point and a column for each neuron. The computation that forms it from X and W is called the matrix product and is written like a multiplication: Z=XW+bH=tanh⁡(Z) The bias is added to every row, and tanh acts on each number by itself. Every further layer is the same line again, with H instead of X as its input. The whole forward computation of our network is three such lines. Watch the matrix product as an animation. For a small example, X with three rows and W with three columns, one number of Z appears after the other: row i and column j light up, then the two pairs of numbers that are multiplied, and below them the calculation runs along. With One step you can work it out with it at your own pace. What do we gain by this? The computation is exactly the same: the same multiplications and additions in the same order, and the program of this chapter arrives number for number at the field's result. Gained are three things. The program becomes clearer: the forward computation is three lines, and the number of points no longer appears in them. The matrix product is the one computation processors and graphics cards are built for; large matrices spread well over the cores and onto the card. With our 200 points and four neurons none of this shows yet, the matrices are tiny. And it prepares the next chapter: a program that forms derivatives by itself then needs a rule for a handful of matrix operations instead of for every single multiplication."],
["matrices.htm","matrices-backprop","3.2","Backpropagation with Matrices","Backwards it works the same way. In chapter 2 every neuron had, for every point, its share of the error, the error after its sum. Write these shares one under the other as well, a row for each point and a column for each neuron, and they make a matrix D the size of Z: dij is neuron j's share of the error for point i. For the output it is a single column, row by row the familiar (a−t)(1−a2). One layer further forward, neuron k gets its share through all the weights with which the neurons of the layer behind read its answer, and through its own tanh: d′ik=(∑jdijwkj)(1−hik2) With a single output the sum has one term, and that is the dvj of chapter 2. The sum itself is a matrix product again: row i of D with row k of W. To make that a row times a column, W is mirrored, its rows becoming columns; that is called the transpose WT. The tanh acts on each number on its own, number times number at the same place, written ⊙: D′=(DWT)⊙(1−H2) What remains are the derivatives of the weights. In chapter 2 every point added to weight vj the product dhj: the answer the weight reads, times the share of the neuron that reads it. Added up over all points and divided by their number, that was the derivative. For wkj it is the same, point i adds hikdij, and the sum over all points is row k of HT times column j of D: ∑ihikdij=(HTD)kj All these sums together are the matrix product HTD, the size of W, one number for each weight. The bias reads a 1 instead of an answer, so its sums are simply the column sums of D. With that a whole learning step is written in matrices: forwards, each layer's Z and H; backwards, each layer's D, from it HTD and the column sums; and then every weight is decreased by its derivative as before, divided by the number of points and times the learning rate. In the program of this chapter each of these formulas is a function. back makes from the D of a layer, its weights W and the answers H of the layer before it that layer's D′: function back(D: Matrix, W: Matrix, H: Matrix) returns Matrix { var below zeros(D.rows, W.rows) for i in D.rows { var k 0 while k < below.cols { var s 0.0 var j 0 while j < W.cols { s := s + D.at(i, j) * W.at(k, j) j := j + 1 } let out H.at(i, k) below.put(i, k, s * (1.0 - out * out)) k := k + 1 } } below } The outer loop goes over the points i, the middle one over the neurons k of the layer before, the inner one makes the sum over j. In doing so it reads row k of W, and that is column k of WT: the transpose is never written down, it is enough to read W the other way round. And the ⊙ is the multiplication in below.put, each sum times the slope of its own tanh. weight_grad is the matrix product HTD in the same pattern; here too HT is never written down, the inner loop reads column k of H. bias_grad adds up the columns of D, out_share makes the D of the output, and descend decreases every number of a matrix by its derivative. That makes the whole learning step: procedure step(mutable net: Net, X: Matrix, T: Matrix, rate: f64) { ; forward, layer by layer let H1 layer(X, net.W1, net.b1) let H2 layer(H1, net.W2, net.b2) let A layer(H2, net.W3, net.b3) ; backward, layer by layer let D3 out_share(A, T) let D2 back(D3, net.W3, H2) let D1 back(D2, net.W2, H1) ; every weight a little against its gradient descend(net.W3, weight_grad(H2, D3), rate, X.rows) descend(net.b3, bias_grad(D3), rate, X.rows) descend(net.W2, weight_grad(H1, D2), rate, X.rows) descend(net.b2, bias_grad(D2), rate, X.rows) descend(net.W1, weight_grad(X, D1), rate, X.rows) descend(net.b1, bias_grad(D1), rate, X.rows) } At the top the three lines forward, below them the three backward, from the output to the input, because each D comes from the D of the layer behind it. Only when all the D are there do the weights change: back needs the old ones. The points appear in these lines only as X.rows, their number, which descend divides by. The whole program is on GitHub."],
["matrices.htm","matrices-memory","3.3","Where the Numbers Live","In the program a matrix is a record of its two sizes and a Vector[f64] with its numbers, row after row. Rows and columns count from 0 there, and the number in row i and column j is at place i·n+j when the matrix has n columns: define Matrix(rows: int, cols: int, data: Vector[f64]) { function at(this, i: int, j: int) returns f64 this.data[i * this.cols + j] procedure put(mutable this, i: int, j: int, v: f64) this.data.put(i * this.cols + j, v) } function zeros(rows: int, cols: int) returns Matrix { var data Vector[f64]#(rows * cols) for k in rows * cols data.put(k, 0.0) Matrix(rows, cols, data) } zeros makes a matrix in which every number is 0. The # after the type says where its numbers live; more on that in a moment. A learning step makes quite a few such matrices: forward the answers of the three layers, backward the three D, and the derivatives of the weights. Together that is a good 2800 numbers or 22 KB a step, 1.1 GB for 50,000 steps. Only one step of it is ever needed: once the weights have changed, everything else is out of date. Other languages clear such intermediate values away with a garbage collector, which looks from time to time at what the program can still reach; in C you give each one back with free. Scaly does neither. What a call makes lives on a memory page of that call, and when it returns, the whole page is freed at once. What it returns must outlive that, so it makes it right away on its caller's page. That is the # in zeros. That layer, back and the other functions return their result from zeros, the compiler sees by itself, and it hands the page on. So all the matrices of a learning step live on the page of step, and that is freed when step returns. Only the weights stay: the main program made them, and descend writes into them. Measured, the program needs at most 2.08 MB of memory with 500 steps, and just as much with 50,000 steps. The learning steps come and go, and the memory does not grow with them. There is one trap. The main program has a page too, and it lives until the end. Write the output of the losses straight into the main loop, and the answers it computes for it lie on that page and stay: after 50,000 steps, with an output every 100 steps, that is 18.7 MB. So the output is a procedure of its own, and what comes about in it goes with it: procedure report(step: int, net: Net, X: Matrix, T: Matrix, TX: Matrix, TT: Matrix) { let train loss(answer(net, X), T) let test loss(answer(net, TX), TT) print \"step `step` train `train` test `test`\" }"],
["matrices.htm","matrices-fast","3.4","Fast","That leaves the question of how fast all this is. For our circle it is hardly worth asking: built with scaly build --release, the program takes a hundredth of a second for its 500 learning steps. With images it is another matter; the handwritten digits of MNIST are 60,000 images of 784 points each. How the time grows with the size shows in the matrix product on its own, C=AB for two square matrices of n rows and columns: function product(A: Vector[f32], B: Vector[f32], n: int) returns Vector[f32] { var C Vector[f32]#((n * n) as size_t) for t in n * n { let i t / n let j t % n var s: f32 0.0 var k 0 while k < n { let a A[(i * n + k) as size_t] s := s + a * B[(k * n + j) as size_t] k := k + 1 } C.put(t as size_t, s) } C } A round of the loop makes one number of C, row i of A times column j of B. Here the matrices are just their Vector, row after row, and their numbers are f32 instead of f64: they take half the memory, and that is how a graphics card computes. The time for one product, measured on a MacBook Pro with an M1 Pro, with one core, with all ten, on the graphics card, and as Scaly decides by itself: none coreall corescardScaly decides640.18 ms0.10 ms0.33 ms0.12 ms1281.7 ms0.29 ms0.43 ms0.33 ms25615.6 ms2.0 ms1.06 ms1.7 ms512127 ms17.9 ms5.0 ms5.0 ms10241.08 s0.20 s35 ms35 ms204825.4 s8.0 s0.27 s0.27 s With one core the time grows like n3: twice the size is eight times the work. Only from 1024 to 2048 it takes about 24 times as long. The numbers of a column of B lie far apart in memory, each n places after the one before, and at 2048 they no longer fit into the processor's fast intermediate memory, the cache; it then mostly waits for memory. All cores together are faster from 64 on, at 1024 a good five times. The graphics card overtakes them between 128 and 256, and at 2048 it computes thirty times as fast as all the cores. The last column is the one that matters. The program says only for; where it goes, Scaly decides while it runs: it measures what a round costs and hands the loop to the cores or to the card where that pays. It makes the faster choice; only at 256, where cores and card are close, it switches between the two and ends up in between. And everywhere the result is the same, bit for bit: the card adds up every sum in the same order as the processor. The loops of our circle program are of the same kind. Each round fills one row of the matrix it writes, and so that Scaly sees this, the inner loops run up to that matrix's number of columns: while j < H.cols in layer. So Scaly spreads all six loops over the points across the cores by itself as soon as that pays; with 200 points it does not pay yet. The whole measuring program is on GitHub."],
["tape.htm","","4","The Tape","In chapter 3 we derived every derivative by hand and wrote a function for each. In this chapter the program forms them itself: we write only the forward computation, the way the formulas read, and the way back follows from it."],
["tape.htm","tape-values","4.1","Values That Remember Where They Came From","The backward computation of chapter 3 follows a pattern. Every step forward has a step backward, and which one depends only on what was computed going forward: a matrix product, a sum, a tanh. If every value remembers how it came about, a program can walk that way back without our writing it down. This is called automatic differentiation, and the record it walks is called the tape. In our program every value has an entry for it: define Entry(kind: int, a: ref[Entry]?, b: ref[Entry]?, value: Matrix, var grad: Matrix, var seen: bool) kind says what was computed, a and b are the entries the value came from, and value is the value itself, a matrix. The question mark after ref[Entry] means the reference may be missing: a tanh has only an a, and the points and the weights the computation starts from have none. grad is as large as value and takes the derivatives on the way back; seen is needed there too. For a matrix A we write these derivatives as a matrix GA of the same size: (GA)ij=∂L/∂aij The number at place i, j says how strongly the loss L changes when aij changes a little. The D of chapter 3 is such a matrix, a layer's GZ. A value of the computation is a reference to its entry, and for values we write the operators * and + ourselves: define Value(at: ref[Entry]) { operator *(this, other: Value) returns Value { let m product(this.at.value, other.at.value) Value(&# Entry(TIMES, this.at, other.at, m, zeros(m.rows, m.cols), false)) } operator +(this, other: Value) returns Value { let m plus(this.at.value, other.at.value) Value(&# Entry(PLUS, this.at, other.at, m, zeros(m.rows, m.cols), false)) } } Each computes with the matrices of its two values, with product, the matrix product, and with plus, which adds one row to every row like the bias. For the result it makes a new entry that points to the two. Matrices the computation starts from become values through leaf, and tanh is a function that makes an entry like the operators do: function leaf(m: Matrix) returns Value Value(&# Entry(LEAF, null, null, m, zeros(m.rows, m.cols), false)) function tanh(x: Value) returns Value { let m apply_tanh(x.at.value) Value(&# Entry(TANH, x.at, null, m, zeros(m.rows, m.cols), false)) } With them a layer is written like its formulas Z=XW+b and H=tanh⁡(Z), in one line: tanh(leaf(X) * w1 + b1). Libraries such as PyTorch keep the tape as a list: every computation appends itself while it runs. In Scaly that does not work. Operators and functions are pure; they return their result and change nothing else, no list outside either. So here every entry points to the ones it came from. Together the entries form a graph that reaches from the loss to the points and the weights, and the order, the tape proper, is made from it only on the way back. That leaves the question where the entries live. &# Entry(…) makes an entry and, with &, hands out a reference to it. The # says where, as in zeros: on the caller's page. The operators and tanh are called in step, so all the entries of a learning step lie on its page and go with it, like the matrices in chapter 3. No number is copied on the way: handing on a matrix hands on its sizes and the reference to its numbers, so the entry leaf(net.W1) makes sees the same numbers as the network."],
["tape.htm","tape-back","4.2","The Way Back","At the end of the forward computation stands the loss L, a matrix with a single number, and its derivative by itself is 1. From there the way leads back: every entry hands its derivatives on to the entries it came from. It may do so only once every entry that reads it has given it its share. order puts the entries in an order for that, each after the ones it came from, and backward walks it from the end to the front: procedure order(mutable e: ref[Entry], mutable tape: Array[ref[Entry]]) { if e.seen return e.seen := true let a e.a if a <> null order(a, tape) let b e.b if b <> null order(b, tape) tape.add(e) } procedure backward(mutable loss: Value) { var tape Array[ref[Entry]]() order(loss.at, tape) loss.at.grad.put(0, 0, 1.0) var k tape.length while k > 0 { k := k - 1 back(tape[k]) } } order goes to a and b first and appends the entry itself only after them. seen makes sure that an entry two computations read comes onto the tape only once. In our network every value is read by just one computation, but this way backward is right for any. What an entry hands on depends only on its kind. For the matrix product: C=ABGA=GCBT,GB=ATGC These are the two products of chapter 3: GCBT is the DWT that hands the loss one layer forward, and ATGC is the HTD, the derivative of the weights. The sum C=A+b hands GC on unchanged, to A and to b; since b was added to every row, it gets the column sums, like the bias in chapter 3. For tanh each number is multiplied by the slope at its place: H=tanh⁡(Z)GZ=GH⊙(1−H2) And for the loss, the mean over the n points: L=1n∑i12(ai−ti)2∂L/∂ai=(ai−ti)/n One after the other these rules give the formulas of chapter 3. The loss hands (a−t)/n to the network's answer, its tanh makes (a−t)(1−a2)/n of it, the D of the output, here already divided by the number of points. The sum hands it on to the product and to b3, the product to h2 and to w3, and so on down to the points. When two computations read the same value, two derivatives arrive at its entry, and they add up. That is why the rules in the program add to grad instead of setting it. back picks the rule by the entry's kind: procedure back(mutable e: ref[Entry]) { let a e.a if a = null return if e.kind = TANH back_tanh(e.value, e.grad, a) let b e.b if b = null return if e.kind = TIMES back_times(e.grad, a, b) if e.kind = PLUS back_plus(e.grad, a, b) if e.kind = LOSS back_loss(e.grad, a, b) } Each rule is a small procedure. The one for tanh: procedure back_tanh(H: Matrix, G: Matrix, mutable a: ref[Entry]) { for k in H.rows * H.cols { let h H.data[k] a.grad.data.put(k, a.grad.data[k] + G.data[k] * (1.0 - h * h)) } } And this is the whole learning step: procedure step(mutable net: Net, X: Matrix, T: Matrix, rate: f64) { let w1 leaf(net.W1) let b1 leaf(net.b1) let w2 leaf(net.W2) let b2 leaf(net.b2) let w3 leaf(net.W3) let b3 leaf(net.b3) let h1 tanh(leaf(X) * w1 + b1) let h2 tanh(h1 * w2 + b2) let a tanh(h2 * w3 + b3) var l loss(a, leaf(T)) backward(l) descend(net.W1, w1, rate) descend(net.b1, b1, rate) descend(net.W2, w2, rate) descend(net.b2, b2, rate) descend(net.W3, w3, rate) descend(net.b3, b3, rate) } The three lines forward read like the formulas, and the backward computation, three lines and four functions in chapter 3, is one call of backward. After it the entry of every weight holds its derivative, and descend moves the weight by it, times the learning rate. Another network, with one more layer or another activation, needs no new backward computation, only a rule for each new kind of computation; the last section tries it. The whole program is on GitHub."],
["tape.htm","tape-same","4.3","The Same Numbers","Does the tape compute right? This chapter's program learns the circle from the same starting weights and with the same learning rate as the one of chapter 3, so it must print the same losses. Nearly all numbers agree digit for digit; three differ: StepChapter 3Tape100, training0.483253082286431430.48325308228643155300, test0.398500796376129840.3985007963761298500, training0.19223560940857510.19223560940857506 The difference is in the 16th or 17th digit, and it has nothing to do with the tape: the program computes in another order. Chapter 3 begins every sum with the bias, the tape adds it last. Chapter 3 divides the finished derivative by the number of points, the tape divides every share before adding it. Mathematically that is the same, but a floating-point number has only 53 binary digits, and every operation rounds to them. In another order the rounding falls in other places. The outcome does not change: both programs get 188 of the 200 test points right. The tape is not free. It makes a second matrix for every value, and it also forms derivatives nobody needs, the ones by the points. For 50,000 learning steps it takes 1.65 seconds instead of 1.02, both built with scaly build --release, on the same M1 Pro. Memory does not grow all the same: at most 2.06 MB, for 500 steps as for 50,000, because the entries go with the page of step."],
["tape.htm","tape-relu","4.4","Another Activation","What does the tape bring if it computes the same numbers more slowly? It pays off as soon as the network changes. In the two hidden layers, let us take instead of tanh the activation chapter 2 mentioned for large networks, ReLU: H=max⁡(0,Z) The output stays tanh; it is still to answer between −1 and 1. With the tape ReLU needs what tanh needs: a function that makes an entry, and a rule for the way back. function relu(x: Value) returns Value { let m apply_relu(x.at.value) Value(&# Entry(RELU, x.at, null, m, zeros(m.rows, m.cols), false)) } apply_relu sets every negative number to 0. The slope of ReLU is 1 where the sum is positive, and 0 elsewhere. So the rule hands GH on unchanged where zij>0, and nothing elsewhere. Whether the sum was positive it sees from the answer, since ReLU gives more than 0 exactly there: procedure back_relu(H: Matrix, G: Matrix, mutable a: ref[Entry]) { for k in H.rows * H.cols { if H.data[k] > 0.0 a.grad.data.put(k, a.grad.data[k] + G.data[k]) } } Add a line in back that calls this rule for the kind RELU, and in the learning step tanh becomes relu in two places: relu(leaf(X) * w1 + b1). That is all. Without the tape there would be more to think about. In chapter 3 the slope of tanh sits in two places of the derived backward computation: as 1−h2 in back, for the hidden layers, and in out_share, for the output. We would have to know which of them becomes the slope of ReLU and which stays, and for an operation that does not simply act number by number, derive the chain anew. With the tape every rule stays with its operation, and how the rules chain follows from the network. Whether the new rule is right can be checked against the field of chapter 2: its program can do ReLU too, with a backward computation written by hand. From the same starting weights this section's program prints the same losses as the field, again up to the last places. It also shows that ReLU learns differently here. After 500 steps only 132 of the 200 test points are right, after 2000 steps 157, and the loss falls only from 0.51 to 0.33 in that time. Then it goes fast: after 2500 steps all 200 are right. With tanh that was so after 1000 steps already. Which activation is better depends on the network and the data, and trying it now takes one word. The program with ReLU is on GitHub."],
];
